ed. The utterance is not framed as a question, request, command, apology, or other distinct act.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"After Nikki and Keith have had sex and are lying together in bed, Nikki turns to Keith with her “heart full” and suddenly tells him that she loves him. Keith pauses, smiles, reciprocates the avowal, and they kiss.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"Nikki’s focal utterance contains no explicit undertaking or identifiable terms of commitment. Keith reciprocates the love avowal, so it receives affirmative interpersonal uptake, but that response does not establish that Nikki promised a future course of action or formalized their relationship.\",\"recognised_work\":false,\"recognition_note\":\"I do not recognize the supplied passage as a specific known work.\",\"limitations\":\"The supplied scene strongly supports interpreting the utterance as an honest and spontaneous avowal, but it does not establish any precise relationship status, long-term commitment, or agreed future obligations arising from the focal words.\"}"
